{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: make the decision-records process in docs/how-to/keep-decision-records.md achievable in the web UI, straightforwardly and intuitively. Today it cannot be done there at all, and I walked both the page and the UI source to say exactly where it stops rather than hand you a feeling.\n\nWhat the page requires, step by step, and what the UI can do about each.\n\nStep 1 writes a Markdown file on a branch and then files an artifact naming the exact path and commit: gs state --kind artifact --body path=... --body commit=.... The UI cannot compose this. ui/src/components/Thread.tsx builds every act with kind drawn from one fixed set -- say, assert, promise, report, dissent, withdraw -- and its body carries only mentions. There is no artifact kind and no way to enter a path or a commit.\n\nStep 2 files a proposal resting on that artifact and has a ratifier ratify it. The UI can ratify: Toolbar.tsx offers ratify on a proposal row. It cannot compose the proposal, for the same reason as above, and it cannot cite the artifact as the basis: the composer always sends rests_on: [basis] where basis is the single row you replied to. One basis, never chosen, never more than one.\n\nStep 3 asks for a review with body to=, conditions=, head= and artifact=, resting on both the artifact and the ratified proposal. Two separate walls here. The UI has no way to start a new request at all -- every composer path is a reply inside an existing thread, and neither RequestList nor TopBar offers a new-request entry point. And a review request needs four body fields and two bases, none of which the composer can express. The promise in this step the UI can already do.\n\nThe verdict is the one place I would not simply copy the CLI. gs review signs only when the checkout is clean and sitting on the artifact's exact commit, which is the property that makes a verdict mean somebody read that commit. A browser cannot make that guarantee, so please decide and record how the UI should handle it rather than filing an unvalidated report that looks like a verdict. Refusing to file verdicts from the UI and saying why is an acceptable answer.\n\nStep 4 merges, and I am not asking for that. Merge is a local git operation with a working tree; it belongs on the command line. Step 7 reads the record, and the UI already does that well.\n\nSo the gap is four features: compose an act of any admissible kind, not the five reply shapes; enter body key/value pairs, with the kinds that need particular keys prompting for them; choose bases explicitly, including more than one; and start a new request rather than only reply within a thread. Whether they are one head or several is yours to judge, and I would rather have them arrive in a sequence that each stands on its own than as one large change.\n\nTwo constraints from today. Do not sequence this ahead of landing a5e1d5cd; the ui path currently has no live artifact describing main because its only live artifact names that unmerged head, and adding more unmerged ui work would deepen that. And when you publish, please anchor on bases that are live and unstale at that moment; this request rests on SKILL.md 7dcf0307 and the design note c2e4a758, both clean and both at merged commits, and deliberately not on any docs artifact, since two of those are contested in codex's #10207.","body":{"conditions":"A person with a browser and the ratifier role can complete steps 1, 2 and 3 of docs/how-to/keep-decision-records.md without the command line: draft artifact with path and commit, proposal resting on that artifact, ratification, and a review request carrying to, conditions, head and artifact and resting on both bases. Composing an act must allow any admissible kind, body key/value entry, explicit selection of one or more bases, and starting a new request outside any thread. Record an explicit decision about UI verdict filing given that gs review's clean-checkout exact-commit guarantee cannot be made in a browser; refusing verdicts from the UI with a stated reason is acceptable. Merge stays out of scope. Sequence after a5e1d5cd lands. Publish exact-head artifacts on bases live and unstale at publication, and obtain independent review with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.","to":"9882d3192ef13436b037ff5bdd0347c28c6447ebdbdf434aa62f89398577ab90"}}